# This file sets nothing, and the reason is the point of the scenario.
#
# EAP-TLS against a stock strongSwan, which lands on TLS 1.2 by default
# (`version_max = 1.2`, /etc/strongswan.d/charon.conf) and implements no
# RFC 7627 Extended Master Secret: `extended_master_secret` appears in
# strongSwan 5.9.14 only as a name in a log table, never in tls_peer.c or
# tls_server.c.
#
# RFC 5216 Section 2.3 defines the EAP-TLS MSK as a crypto/tls
# ExportKeyingMaterial result, and Go refuses that export when the session is
# below TLS 1.3 AND did not negotiate RFC 7627 (crypto/tls, conn.go:
# `c.vers != VersionTLS13 && !c.extMasterSecret`). Both halves hold here, so ze
# cannot authenticate this peer.
#
# This file used to set the `tlsunsafeekm` GODEBUG setting, which lifted the
# refusal for
# this scenario alone rather than for every ze binary (Thomas, 2026-08-01). Go
# 1.27 REMOVED that setting. The runtime raises a fatal error before main() when
# a removed key carries its old value, so the line stopped ze at container start
# and the scenario failed with no IKE packet sent. Restoring it is not an option:
# there is no replacement, and no supported way to reach this peer over TLS 1.2.
#
# What ze now does instead is report the refusal: exportEAPTLSMSK
# (internal/core/eap) names the peer, the negotiated version, RFC 7627
# and the operator's answers, and handleEAPResponse
# (internal/component/ike/engine) logs it.
#
# Scenario eap-tls13 is the same exchange over TLS 1.3 and carries no ze-env
# file at all, which is what proves the TLS 1.3 path needs no weakening.
